WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.272
This example is intentionally incorrect, and the error is useful.

00:00:04.272 --> 00:00:11.117
The query groups rows only by diagnosis but also tries to select subject ID directly.

00:00:11.117 --> 00:00:18.327
PostgreSQL asks an important question: which subject ID should represent the entire diagnosis group?

00:00:18.327 --> 00:00:23.777
There is no single correct subject ID for a group that may contain several patients.

00:00:23.777 --> 00:00:26.877
Run this example and read the error message.

00:00:26.877 --> 00:00:30.437
PostgreSQL is protecting you from an ambiguous result.

00:00:30.437 --> 00:00:35.377
To fix a grouped query, every selected expression needs a clear role.

00:00:35.377 --> 00:00:42.452
It should either define the group, such as diagnosis, or summarize the group, such as COUNT or AVG.

00:00:42.452 --> 00:00:45.437
Do not treat grouping errors as obstacles.

00:00:45.437 --> 00:00:49.727
They often reveal that the result grain has not been defined clearly enough.

00:00:49.727 --> 00:00:54.737
Connect this idea to a dashboard summarizing admissions by department and month.

00:00:54.737 --> 00:01:00.042
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:01:00.042 --> 00:01:07.497
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:01:07.497 --> 00:01:13.582
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:01:13.582 --> 00:01:19.337
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
